A plan with **cuts and releases together**, which the corpus had no case for until
`A1-240` (found by `p00016`, which authored eleven cuts under three releases).

ADR-0022 says a release is a checkpoint over cuts rather than a cut holding work, and
that "a task stays in exactly one cut, because a release claims none of them". The
model honoured that and the reporting did not: a release carries its cuts' members for
its own rollup (`A1-146`), and every surface that asked "which cut is this task in"
read that borrowed list as ownership.

The cuts and the release are counted apart, and the release names what it closes
rather than repeating the rows of the cuts it closes:

```
$ aplan roadmap
roadmap: 2 cut(s), 1 release(s), 2 task(s), 3.0d effort

v0.1 (release)  The first release  [todo]  0/2 done, 2 ready, 0 blocked, crit 2.0d, 3.0d effort
  closes s0 (M-s0)
  closes s1 (M-s1)

s0  First cut  [todo]  0/1 done, 1 ready, 0 blocked, crit 1.0d, 1.0d effort
  IP-1        todo          Work in the first cut

s1  Second cut  [todo]  0/1 done, 1 ready, 0 blocked, crit 2.0d, 2.0d effort
  IP-2        todo          Work in the second cut

(statuses are as claimed in the plan and gated by `aplan verify` in CI)

```

The task count is the cuts' work counted once: three effort days over two tasks, not
six over four. A release borrowing its cuts' rollup is what makes `0/2 done` an answer
to "how is v0.1 going" rather than `0/0`, and it is why the double-count was easy to
introduce.

`tasks` is the surface a rollup is written against, and it reports the cut a task is
**in**. Every row names both, so the association is pinned rather than inferred: the
release is in no cut, because it is not in one.

```
$ aplan tasks
id            status      effort  cut       ready   title
IP-1          todo          1.0  s0        yes     Work in the first cut
IP-2          todo          2.0  s1        yes     Work in the second cut
M-s0          todo            -  s0        -       First cut
M-s1          todo            -  s1        -       Second cut
M-v01         todo            -  -         -       The first release

```

Before `A1-240` the `cut` column read `v0.1` for every row, including the two cuts: the
walk returned the first entry in roadmap order that listed the task, and a release lists
its cuts' members. A `jq` line over this surface was wrong on any plan with a release,
which is the whole reason `tasks` exists (ADR-0008, `A1-182`).
